      "In 399 BCE the Athenian philosopher Socrates, then about seventy, was tried before a popular jury of around five hundred citizens. His accusers were Meletus, a young poet, Anytus, a leading democratic politician, and Lycon, an orator. The charge was that he did not recognize the gods the city recognized, introduced new divine beings, and corrupted the young.",
      "Our main sources are two of his followers, Plato and Xenophon, both writing after his death and both eager to defend him. In Plato's Apology Socrates says he has spent his life questioning those reputed to be wise, exposing that they did not know what they thought they knew, and that he follows a divine sign, his daimonion, that warns him away from certain actions. He tells the jury that if they released him on condition that he stop philosophizing, he would obey the god rather than them.",
      "He was convicted by a fairly narrow margin; in Plato's account he remarks that if about thirty votes had gone the other way he would have been acquitted. Asked to propose a penalty, he first suggested free meals in the Prytaneum, an honour for public benefactors, and then a fine that his friends, including Plato, would guarantee. The jury voted for death, and he died by drinking hemlock.",
      "The political setting matters. Athens had lost the Peloponnesian War in 404. A pro-Spartan oligarchy, the Thirty Tyrants, then ruled by terror until democracy was restored in 403. Critias, a leader of the Thirty, and Alcibiades, who had defected to Sparta during the war, had both been associates of Socrates. An amnesty of 403 barred prosecutions for political acts committed before it, which may explain why the charge was framed as impiety.",
      "In Plato's Crito, his friend Crito arranges an escape. Socrates refuses, imagining the Laws of Athens arguing that by living in the city all his life he had agreed to abide by its judgments, and that to escape would be to try to destroy the laws. Readers have long asked how this fits with his statement in the Apology that he would disobey an order to stop philosophizing.",
      "Historians disagree about why he was really tried. Some, like the journalist I. F. Stone in The Trial of Socrates (1988), argued that the motive was political: Socrates was seen as a critic of democracy and teacher of its enemies. Others take the religious charge seriously, noting that Athenians believed impiety could bring divine anger on the whole city. Xenophon even suggested that Socrates spoke defiantly because he preferred death to the hardships of old age."
