      "The Peloponnesian War (431-404 BCE) was fought between Athens, with its maritime empire, and Sparta and its allies. Its historian, the Athenian general Thucydides, wrote that the truest cause of the war, though the least spoken of, was the growth of Athenian power and the fear this caused in Sparta. He set this against the immediate disputes over Corcyra and Potidaea.",
      "In 416 BCE Athens sent a force to the island of Melos, a Spartan colony that had stayed out of the Athenian empire. Thucydides presents the negotiation as a dialogue between Athenian envoys and the Melian magistrates. The Athenians refuse to argue about justice, saying that justice applies only between equals in power, and that the strong do what they can and the weak suffer what they must.",
      "The Melians appeal to fairness, to the gods who they hope will favour the just, to Sparta's obligation to help its colony, and to the hope that fortune may turn. The Athenians answer that the gods and men alike rule wherever they can, that Sparta will not risk itself for Melos, and that hope is a costly comfort for those who have nothing else. The Melians refuse to submit.",
      "Athens besieged the city. When it surrendered, the Athenians killed the men of military age, enslaved the women and children, and later settled five hundred colonists there. Thucydides places the dialogue directly before his account of the Sicilian Expedition of 415-413, in which Athens lost a great fleet and army.",
      "Thucydides said his speeches give what the speakers would have needed to say on each occasion, kept as close as possible to the general sense of what was actually said. The words are his composition, so readers must ask what he meant by them.",
      "Interpretations divide. Many international relations scholars treat Thucydides as the founder of political realism, for whom power and fear, not justice, drive states. Others read the dialogue as tragedy: Athenian arrogance is shown just before its punishment in Sicily, and Thucydides is criticizing, not endorsing, the Athenian view. The political scientist Graham Allison coined the phrase Thucydides Trap for the danger of war when a rising power threatens a ruling one, a use of Thucydides that some classicists dispute."
