    "passage": [
      "In 1789 a royal financial crisis led Louis XVI to summon the Estates-General. The Third Estate declared itself the National Assembly, crowds in Paris stormed the Bastille on 14 July, and in August the Assembly abolished feudal privileges and adopted the Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen, which proclaimed that men are born and remain free and equal in rights.",
      "From 1792 the Revolution radicalized. France went to war with Austria and then Prussia, the monarchy was overthrown in August 1792, a republic was declared, and the king was tried and executed in January 1793. Revolts broke out in the Vendée and in several cities, and foreign armies threatened the frontiers.",
      "In 1793-1794 the Committee of Public Safety, in which Maximilien Robespierre was a leading figure, governed through what came to be called the Terror. The Law of Suspects of September 1793 allowed broad arrests, and the Law of 22 Prairial in June 1794 stripped defendants of most protections. Around 17,000 people were officially executed, many more died in prison, and tens of thousands more were killed in the repression of the Vendée. Robespierre himself was overthrown and executed in July 1794, on 9 Thermidor.",
      "Contemporaries argued about the Revolution from the start. Edmund Burke, in Reflections on the Revolution in France (1790), warned that tearing down inherited institutions in the name of abstract rights would end in violence and despotism. Thomas Paine replied in Rights of Man that hereditary government had no rightful claim and that the Revolution restored natural rights.",
      "Historians have offered competing explanations of the Terror. The circumstances thesis holds that war, civil war and economic crisis drove an embattled government to emergency measures. François Furet and others argued instead that the Terror grew from the Revolution's own political ideas: a single sovereign people whose will had to be unified, so that opponents became enemies of the people.",
      "Historians also disagree about what kind of revolution it was. Marxist historians such as Georges Lefebvre and Albert Soboul saw a bourgeois revolution in which a rising capitalist class overthrew feudal aristocracy. Revisionists, beginning with Alfred Cobban, argued that the revolutionaries were mostly lawyers and officials rather than capitalists, and that the Revolution is better explained by political conflict than by class."
