      "The Enlightenment was an eighteenth-century movement of thinkers who sought to apply reason and critical inquiry to religion, politics, law and society. In 1784 Immanuel Kant defined enlightenment as humanity's emergence from its self-incurred immaturity, and gave it the motto Sapere aude: dare to know, have the courage to use your own understanding.",
      "Its figures differed widely. Montesquieu, in The Spirit of the Laws (1748), argued for separating legislative, executive and judicial powers. Voltaire attacked religious intolerance. Rousseau, in The Social Contract (1762), grounded legitimate government in the general will. Cesare Beccaria's On Crimes and Punishments (1764) attacked torture and the death penalty. Diderot and d'Alembert edited the Encyclopédie, and David Hume and Adam Smith worked in Scotland.",
      "They did not agree about reason itself. Hume argued that reason is, and ought only to be, the slave of the passions: it can tell us how to reach our ends but cannot by itself set them. Rousseau criticized the arts and sciences as corrupting. Some historians therefore speak of several Enlightenments rather than one; Jonathan Israel distinguished a radical Enlightenment, which he traced to Spinoza, from a moderate one.",
      "Enlightenment ideals of universal rights were proclaimed while slavery and colonial rule continued, and many of its thinkers excluded women or held racial hierarchies. Others, such as Diderot in his contributions to Raynal's history of the two Indies, condemned colonial conquest and slavery. Critics disagree about whether these failures expose the ideals as hollow or show that the ideals were applied inconsistently.",
      "Critics have also blamed the Enlightenment for later disasters. Max Horkheimer and Theodor Adorno, in Dialectic of Enlightenment (1944), argued that reason, used as a tool of domination over nature and people, turned into its opposite. Isaiah Berlin studied the Counter-Enlightenment thinkers who defended tradition, feeling and particular cultures. Defenders, such as Steven Pinker, credit Enlightenment values with long-term gains in health, knowledge and freedom."
