      "The Cold War was the rivalry between the United States and its allies and the Soviet Union and its allies from the end of the Second World War until the Soviet collapse in 1991. The two superpowers never fought each other directly, but competed through alliances, arms races, propaganda and wars fought by or through others.",
      "Its opening years set the pattern. The Soviet Union established communist governments across Eastern Europe. In his Long Telegram of 1946, the American diplomat George Kennan described Soviet expansionism and recommended containment. The Truman Doctrine (1947) promised support to free peoples resisting subjugation, and the Marshall Plan (1947) funded European recovery. The Berlin Blockade of 1948-1949 and the founding of NATO in 1949 divided Europe.",
      "The conflict spread worldwide: the Korean War (1950-1953), the Cuban Missile Crisis of 1962, the Vietnam War, a period of détente in the 1970s, and the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan in 1979. Millions died in wars connected to the rivalry, mostly outside Europe.",
      "Historians have argued over who was responsible. The orthodox view blamed Soviet expansionism. Revisionists such as William Appleman Williams, in The Tragedy of American Diplomacy (1959), blamed an American drive for open markets and economic expansion. Post-revisionists sought a synthesis, stressing mutual misperception and security fears. After Soviet archives partly opened, John Lewis Gaddis, in We Now Know (1997), put more weight on Stalin and on ideology.",
      "Historians also debate whether the Cold War was driven by ideology, communism against liberal capitalism, or by the ordinary rivalry of two great powers left standing after 1945. Gaddis called the period between the superpowers a long peace, crediting nuclear deterrence with preventing a third world war; critics note the proxy wars and the near catastrophe of 1962."
