      "In Brown v. Board of Education (1954) the U.S. Supreme Court ruled that state-mandated racial segregation in public schools was unconstitutional. Many Southern states resisted, and in 1957 President Eisenhower sent federal troops to protect Black students integrating Central High School in Little Rock, Arkansas.",
      "The Montgomery bus boycott of 1955-1956, begun after Rosa Parks was arrested for refusing to give up her seat, brought Martin Luther King Jr. to national prominence. Sit-ins at lunch counters, starting in Greensboro in 1960, and the Freedom Rides of 1961 spread direct action across the South. In Birmingham in 1963 police turned dogs and fire hoses on young marchers, and King wrote his Letter from Birmingham Jail defending nonviolent civil disobedience against unjust laws.",
      "After the March on Washington in August 1963, Congress passed the Civil Rights Act of 1964, banning segregation in public accommodations and discrimination in employment, and, after the violent attack on marchers at Selma, the Voting Rights Act of 1965.",
      "The movement included many voices. Ella Baker, who helped found the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee, favored grassroots organizing over reliance on charismatic leaders. Malcolm X criticized nonviolence and integration as goals; he was assassinated in 1965. In 1966 Stokely Carmichael popularized the slogan Black Power. Some activists, such as the Deacons for Defense, practiced armed self-defense alongside nonviolent campaigns.",
      "Historians debate how change came. Some emphasize national leaders and federal legislation; others, following Jacquelyn Dowd Hall's idea of a long civil rights movement, trace the struggle back to the 1930s and forward beyond 1965, and outside the South. Gerald Rosenberg argued in The Hollow Hope (1991) that court rulings by themselves produced little change, while Michael Klarman argued Brown provoked a backlash that hardened resistance. Mary Dudziak showed that Cold War concerns about America's image abroad pushed federal officials toward civil rights."
